faq:
  - q: "Should I explain an employment gap on my resume?"
    a: "Not on the resume itself — that space is for accomplishments, not explanations. Address the gap briefly in your cover letter or during the phone screen. The exception: if the gap was productive (caregiving, consulting, education, health recovery that resulted in a clear return date), a one-line parenthetical in the timeline is acceptable: '(Career break — family caregiving)' or '(Freelance consulting).'"
  - q: "How do you hide employment gaps on a resume?"
    a: "You should not try to hide them. Recruiters see gaps immediately and the attempt to conceal one is more suspicious than the gap itself. What you can do: use year-only dates instead of month-year if gaps are under 12 months (e.g., '2021 – 2023' instead of 'January 2021 – March 2022'). This is legitimate and standard. For longer gaps, brief honest framing in the cover letter closes the loop."
  - q: "How long of a job gap is too long?"
    a: "There is no universal threshold. A 6-month gap in 2024 is unremarkable. A 2-year gap requires context. What matters more than length is recency and what happened during the gap. Gaps that happened early in a career matter less than recent ones. Gaps that involved relevant activity (consulting, caregiving, health) are easily contextualized."
  - q: "How do I explain a layoff gap?"
    a: "Directly and without apology. 'Was impacted by a company-wide reduction in force' is sufficient. Then redirect: 'I have used the time to sharpen my skills in [area] and I am now targeting [specific type of role].' Layoffs are common, well-understood, and not disqualifying. Owning them directly signals professionalism."
  - q: "Does an employment gap hurt your chances?"
    a: "Less than candidates think — if the rest of the candidacy is strong. The gap becomes the reason for rejection only when it is combined with weak positioning, unclear targeting, or a story that sounds defensive. Fix the positioning, and the gap becomes a footnote."
